## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Could you implement a pure bitwise variant that does not rely on reverse128?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values with four halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-boundary bit tricks and 128-bit reasoning without loops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit rotation via 64-bit halves\n- Normalizing k to 0..127, masking to 128 bits\n- Pure bitwise/shifts, no branches\n\n## Code Example
```javascript
function rotl128(hi, lo, k) {
  const n = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const kk = BigInt(k) & 127n;
  const mask128 = (1n << 128n) - 1n;
  const val = ((n << kk) | (n >> (128n - kk))) & mask128;
  return {
    hi: Number((val >> 64n) & ((1n << 64n) - 1n)),
    lo: Number(val & ((1n << 64n) - 1n))
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to implement without 128-bit types?\n- How to test edge cases: k=0, k=127, k=64?\n- How to port to languages with limited integer width or without unsigned128 support?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128 bit manipulations and zero edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit tricks
- 128 bit layout as hi and lo
- De Bruijn based ctz for 64 and 32 bit values
- Handling zero input gracefully

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline int ctz64(uint64_t x) {
  if (x == 0) return 64;
  static const int tab[64] = { /* 64 entries */ };
  uint64_t y = x & -x;
  return tab[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ULL) >> 58];
}

int lsbIndex128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  int a = ctz64(lo);
  int b = 64 + ctz64(hi);
  int m = - (int)(lo != 0);
  int res = (a & m) | (b & ~m);
  return (lo | hi) == 0 ? -1 : res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if 128 bit intrinsics are unavailable
- Extend to msbIndex128 with branchless patterns

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to decompose a 128-bit value, apply a 64-bit bit-reversal, and reassemble in swapped halves to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit-reversal via mask/shifts
- 128-bit composite type handling
- Endianness intuition for cross-half reversal

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying known bit-hacks to multi-lane data without branching, simulating performance-critical kernels.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting across lanes
- 128-bit to four 32-bit lanes mapping
- Branchless arithmetic
- Endianness and lane indexing

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}

uint32_t popcountPerLane128(uint128 x) {
  uint32_t lo0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t lo1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t hi0 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t hi1 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);

  uint32_t p0 = popcount32(lo0);
  uint32_t p1 = popcount32(lo1);
  uint32_t p2 = popcount32(hi0);
  uint32_t p3 = popcount32(hi1);

  return (p3 << 24) | (p2 << 16) | (p1 << 8) | p0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 64-bit lanes?
- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with SIMD intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level bitboard thinking, edge masking, and 128-bit manipulation without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 8x16 bitboard mapping in 128 bits (bit i = row*16 + col).
- Horizontal moves use 1-bit shifts with edge masks (COL0/COL15).
- Vertical moves use 16-bit shifts with TOP/BOTTOM masks.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode close to C for clarity
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 expandMask128(uint128 m){
  uint128 left  = (m & ~COL0) >> 1;
  uint128 right = (m & ~COL15) << 1;
  uint128 up    = (m & ~TOP) << 16;
  uint128 down  = (m & ~BOTTOM) >> 16;
  return or128(or128(left, right), or128(up, down));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for an irregular 3x5 tile grid embedded in 128 bits?
- What are the performance implications of branching-free bitboard ops on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
The task validates non-branching bit tricks and understanding of parity.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise folding, XOR, parity, non-branching.
- Safe edge case handling (x==0).
- Extensibility to wider widths.

## Code Example
```javascript
int parity64(uint64_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 1;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 128-bit parity?
- What are the trade-offs vs using a compiler intrinsic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with 128-bit data layout and perform blockwise rearrangements using only bitwise ops. It also checks handling of multi-word operands without branching.

## Key Concepts

- Block masking and extraction
- Cross-word recombination
- Branchless 128-bit manipulation

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 swapAdjacentBlocks128(uint128 x, unsigned block) {
    uint64_t M = (1ULL << block) - 1;
    uint64_t A0 = x.lo & M;
    uint64_t A1 = (x.lo >> block) & M;
    uint64_t A2 = x.hi & M;
    uint64_t A3 = (x.hi >> block) & M;
    uint64_t loP = (A0 << block) | A1;
    uint64_t hiP = (A2 << block) | A3;
    return (uint128){.hi = hiP, .lo = loP};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to non-uniform block counts or dynamic block that isn't divisor of 128?
- How to optimize for block=16 across many values in tight loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft compact, branchless bit-twiddling across a 128-bit type.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit split into hi/lo; XOR fold reduces width.
- Branchless reduction using successive shifts and XORs.
- Final parity is the low bit after folding.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation in C-like syntax for readability
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t t = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  t ^= t >> 32;
  t ^= t >> 16;
  t ^= t >> 8;
  t ^= t >> 4;
  t ^= t >> 2;
  t ^= t >> 1;
  return (unsigned int)(t & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compute the full popcount for 128-bit without branches?
- How does this approach fare on different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) bit counting and handling of 128-bit data without branching.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR-based popcount
- 128-bit data split into 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling tricks

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned pop64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (unsigned)(v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize with SIMD intrinsics on x86 or ARM?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, randoms?

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```
